        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>High-energy, high-atomic-number charged particle radiation (HZE) represents a significant health risk to humans during spaceflight.  Although such radiation has been physically characterized during numerous manned spaceflights, limited attempts to explicitly correlate biological responses with naturally occurring HZE particles have been made.  The purpose of this experiment is to induce controlled levels of DNA and chromosomal damage to somatic and germ-line cells and to determine whether exposure to microgravity perturbs cellular repair and tolerance mechanisms.  Radiation-induced damage will be produced by the active radioisotope 45Calcium, which emits beta particles of energies of up to 250 keV.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>The approach is to irradiate healthy, growing nematode worms with §-particles from identical radioisotope sources inflight (0-g and 1-g control centrifuge) and, in parallel, on the ground.  Before the mission, several strains of nematode larvae are freshly prepared and maintained in source cultures and placed in sample containers stowed at 5°C or ambient temperature in the Biorack.  Some strains are transferred by syringe to unshielded and shielded radioactive tubes and either incubated in the 22°C incubator or in the Biorack 1-g centrifuge.  After various exposure times, the larvae are frozen or returned to Earth at ambient temperature for fixation and histological examination.<br><br>Dose versus response curves are compared for somatic chromosome aberrations and germ-line mutations to determine whether there are modifications in the responses arising from altered gravity levels.<br><br>In addition, two containers house worm suspension tubes, without any radioisotopic source, and are exposed to ambient cosmic radiation in order to detect radiation-induced unc-22 mutants; unc-22 mutations were isolated from worms flown on Discovery during the IML-1/STS-42 mission, large deletions of chromatin were detected.  Radiation-induced unc-22 mutant structure is LET (Linear Energy Transfer) dependent.  Two additional containers contain a fem-3 (q20) mutant strain which allows comparisons at an independent chromosomal location.<P><B>Results:</B><P>Initial experimental results suggest that gravity level has little effect upon induced mutation frequency in relation to dose responses. As a result of ambient exposure, the samples contained fem-3 mutants, but, surprisingly, no unc-22 mutants were found. Fem-3 mutants exhibited molecular hybridization and one out of every twenty-six mutants had undergone a large structural change. Despite small sample sizes due to problems with sample recovery, experimental data was consistent with the results of the gravity-independent control.
        </div>

